{"label":"1B","direction":"against","claim":"BillionToOne's cost per test rose ~3% in Q2'26 even as volume rose 35%, so no falling $/Gb is visible in this large NIPT customer's cost line.","detail":"Cost of revenue was $32.341M in Q2'26 vs $23.107M in Q2'25. Delivered/billable tests were ~196K vs ~145K. That gives ~$165 vs ~$159 per test (+3.5%, my arithmetic), matching the filing's stated '+3%'. The filing blames mix toward oncology tests with 'higher costs-per-test'. Samples, supplies, phlebotomy and shipping rose only $4.9M of the $9.2M cost increase, so sequencing reagents are a small part of the cost base.","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2070849/000207084926000050/blln-20260630.htm","date":"2026-08 (Q2'26 10-Q)","source_name":"BillionToOne 10-Q Q2'26","source_type":"filing","quote":"higher costs-per-test","strength":"medium","why_new":"Gives the 10-Q's own cost-per-test percentage and my $/test arithmetic. F312 and F307 covered ASP and margin but not the cost-per-test split."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"against","claim":"BillionToOne's prenatal revenue is still growing ~55% y/y, so NIPT is not maturing at this customer.","detail":"Prenatal revenue was $94.248M in Q2'26 vs $60.852M in Q2'25 (+55%), and $190.8M vs $116.9M for 6M (+63%). Delivered tests grew +35% in Q2 and +40% in 6M. Revenue grew faster than volume because ASP rose 21% in Q2. The 10-Q does not split test counts by prenatal vs oncology. This is a high-growth NIPT account, offsetting the Myriad and Natera women's-health maturity signals.","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2070849/000207084926000050/blln-20260630.htm","date":"2026-08","source_name":"BillionToOne 10-Q Q2'26","source_type":"filing","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"Prenatal revenue growth of 55% in Q2 and 63% in 6M is quantified from segment tables. Earlier items cited total volume only."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"against","claim":"BillionToOne's oncology revenue nearly tripled y/y, so a customer added a sequencing-intensive line while NIPT matures.","detail":"Oncology revenue was $13.663M in Q2'26 vs $4.947M in Q2'25 (+176%), and $24.4M vs $7.1M for 6M. Oncology was 11% of 6M revenue vs 6% a year earlier. The filing says Northstar Select and Northstar Response volumes both rose. Oncology tests carry higher cost per test, which suggests higher Gb per test, though the filing gives no Gb figure.","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2070849/000207084926000050/blln-20260630.htm","date":"2026-08","source_name":"BillionToOne 10-Q Q2'26","source_type":"filing","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"Oncology segment revenue growth is not in the known list."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"against","claim":"BillionToOne's inventory and sequencing-related capex are rising, consistent with continued volume build.","detail":"Inventories were $21.1M at 30 Jun 2026 vs $17.5M at year-end 2025 (+21%), and accrued inventory purchases were $5.3M vs $1.0M. 6M PP&E purchases were $8.5M vs $5.2M. It signed an Austin lease (220,000 sq ft, occupancy 2027) and a Union City lease (~62.7K sq ft, $3.6M/yr base, 12 years). The leases suggest capacity ahead of 2027 volume, though the filing names no sequencer count.","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2070849/000207084926000050/blln-20260630.htm","date":"2026-08","source_name":"BillionToOne 10-Q Q2'26","source_type":"filing","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"Inventory, accrued-inventory and lease figures are new for this customer."},
